ARM: kprobes: reset current_kprobe when a pre-handler returns non-zero

On ARM, kprobe_handler() only calls reset_current_kprobe() inside the branch
taken when the pre-handler returns 0. The non-zero return was reserved for
jprobes, whose break_handler was expected to do the reset on re-entry.

Any other user of the common "modify regs->ARM_pc and return non-zero to say
I redirected execution" idiom therefore leaves the per-CPU current_kprobe
pointer set forever. From that moment kprobe_running() never returns NULL
again, so every later kprobe hit on that CPU takes the "we're recursing"
path: it bumps nmissed, single-steps the probed instruction and continues
WITHOUT EVER CALLING THAT PROBE'S pre_handler.

The failure is silent and very confusing to debug: the breakpoint is still
physically installed (an Oops dump shows 0xde18 at the probe site) and
execution flows straight through it. In practice only the first probe that
redirects works, and every probe registered anywhere in the system is dead
from then on.

Move reset_current_kprobe() out of the conditional so the per-CPU state is
cleared on every path, which is what every other architecture does and what
mainline ended up with after jprobes were removed (jprobes are the only user
that wanted the old behaviour, and nothing here uses them).

--- a/arch/arm/probes/kprobes/core.c
+++ b/arch/arm/probes/kprobes/core.c
@@ -301,8 +301,8 @@
 					kcb->kprobe_status = KPROBE_HIT_SSDONE;
 					p->post_handler(p, regs, 0);
 				}
-				reset_current_kprobe();
 			}
+			reset_current_kprobe();
 		} else {
 			/*
 			 * Probe hit but conditional execution check failed,
